CONTENT = []
A = CONTENT.append

A(('title', 'Integrative Multi-Omics and Multi-Cohort Meta-Analysis Reveal Depleted Butyrate- and Hydrogen-Metabolizing Microbial Consortia and Reduced Fecal Taurine Across the Alzheimer\u2019s Disease Continuum'))

A(('h1', 'Abstract'))
A(('pb', ('Background: ', 'Gut microbial dysbiosis has been linked to Alzheimer\u2019s disease (AD), but studies report inconsistent findings, and whether coordinated microbial\u2013metabolic modules and neuroactive metabolites shift across the AD continuum remains unclear.')))
A(('pb', ('Methods: ', 'We integrated a discovery cohort of 84 participants across the AD continuum (HC, SCD, MCI, AD) with paired 16S rRNA sequencing and fecal metabolomics (117 filtered genera; 1,873 high-confidence metabolites), five validation cohorts (n=755 AD/HC), and two-sample bidirectional Mendelian randomization (MR) using MiBioGen (n=18,340) and IGAP AD (n=63,926) GWAS. Analyses included co-abundance module detection, microbe\u2013metabolite network analysis with permutation testing, random-effects meta-analysis, PICRUSt2 functional prediction, leave-one-cohort-out cross-validation (LOCO-CV), and rank-based mediation analysis with bootstrap inference.')))
A(('pb', ('Results: ', 'A protective Firmicutes-dominated co-abundance module (33 genera, 88% Firmicutes) significantly enriched for short-chain fatty acid (SCFA) producers (odds ratio=7.9, p=3.3\u00d710\u207b\u2074) declined monotonically across the continuum (Spearman r=\u22120.687, p=5.2\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u00b3; MMSE r=+0.673; adjusted for age, sex, and education, r=\u22120.455, p=1.4\u00d710\u207b\u2075), with Blautia the most strongly depleted single genus (HC median 0.28% vs. AD 0.05%, p<10\u207b\u2074) and the hydrogen-consuming acetogenic guild (Blautia and Eubacterium hallii group) depleted in proportion to disease stage (r=\u22120.575, p=1.1\u00d710\u207b\u2078). Fecal taurine was reduced in AD (median normalized intensity 6.46 in HC vs. 4.87 in AD, p=0.006), correlated with MMSE (r=+0.360, p=7.6\u00d710\u207b\u2074), and mediated 19.5% of the Blautia\u2013MMSE association (bootstrap 95% CI 0.0003\u20130.0679, excluding zero). Validation meta-analysis confirmed depletion of 14 genera including multiple butyrate producers (24 FDR-significant in total), reduced aggregate SCFA-producing capacity (pooled d=\u22120.524, p=0.004) corroborated by PICRUSt2 (acetate and propionate biosynthesis FDR<0.05), depletion of fermentative H\u2082 producers (d=\u22120.186, p=0.016, I\u00b2=0%) and H\u2082S producers (d=\u22120.299, p=0.0015), and moderate cross-cohort predictive power (LOCO-CV mean AUC=0.656). Forward MR identified Bifidobacterium as a putative causal risk factor (IVW OR=1.306, 95% CI 1.097\u20131.555, p=0.0027); allele-harmonized reverse MR showed no reverse causation for Bifidobacterium (p=0.626) but a significant effect of AD liability on lower Bacteroides abundance (FDR=0.014).')))
A(('pb', ('Conclusions: ', 'Integrative multi-omics, large-scale meta-analysis, and causal inference converge on depleted butyrate- and hydrogen-metabolizing consortia and reduced fecal taurine in AD, identifying taurine- and hydrogen-centered microbial metabolism as candidate targets for early intervention in the microbiota\u2013gut\u2013brain axis.')))
A(('p', 'Keywords: Alzheimer\u2019s disease; gut microbiome; metabolome; co-abundance network; short-chain fatty acids; butyrate; hydrogen metabolism; taurine; Mendelian randomization; meta-analysis'))

A(('h1', '1. Introduction'))
A(('p', 'Alzheimer\u2019s disease (AD) progresses along a clinical continuum from subjective cognitive decline (SCD) through mild cognitive impairment (MCI) to dementia [11]. The gut microbiome has been implicated in AD pathogenesis via the microbiota\u2013gut\u2013brain axis [8,9,11,34], with cross-sectional studies reporting reduced short-chain fatty acid (SCFA)-producing Firmicutes and expanded pro-inflammatory genera in AD patients [8,14,15]. Germ-free mouse models have shown that the absence of gut microbiota reduces cerebral amyloid deposition [55], supporting a causal role for microbial contributions to neurodegeneration [32,33].'))
A(('p', 'Several issues remain unresolved. Most AD microbiome studies rely on single-taxon differential abundance analysis, which overlooks the higher-order ecological organization of microbial communities and their coordinated metabolic outputs. Network-based approaches such as co-abundance module detection can identify functionally coherent microbe\u2013metabolite sets that vary concordantly across disease states [7], but whether such coordinated modules shift across the AD continuum remains unknown. Moreover, the neuroactive metabolites that could link microbial communities to host cognition\u2014SCFAs, molecular hydrogen (H\u2082), and sulfur-containing amino acids such as taurine\u2014are rarely measured directly alongside community structure.'))
A(('p', 'Individual studies also produce inconsistent findings, likely due to small sample sizes, single-center designs, and technical heterogeneity in sequencing and bioinformatic processing [14,15,30]. A multi-cohort meta-analysis with harmonized processing is needed to identify robust microbial signatures. Furthermore, the cross-cohort predictive value of gut microbial features\u2014particularly SCFA-producing guilds\u2014has not been rigorously evaluated with machine learning frameworks [17].'))
A(('p', 'We address these gaps through a two-stage design: a discovery cohort with paired 16S rRNA sequencing and fecal metabolomics to identify co-abundance modules, the microbe\u2013metabolite network architecture, and disease-associated fecal metabolites (with a focus on taurine as a candidate neuroactive metabolite), followed by a five-cohort validation meta-analysis, complemented by leave-one-cohort-out machine learning, hydrogen-metabolizing guild analysis, PICRUSt2 functional prediction, and bidirectional Mendelian randomization (MR).'))

A(('h1', '2. Materials and Methods'))
A(('h2', '2.1 Study Design'))
A(('p', 'This study used a two-stage design comprising a discovery multi-omics cohort and a multi-cohort validation meta-analysis. The discovery cohort was recruited from a single memory clinic and included 84 participants across four diagnostic groups: healthy controls (HC, n=10), subjective cognitive decline (SCD, n=22), mild cognitive impairment (MCI, n=23), and AD dementia (n=29). Diagnoses were established according to standard clinical criteria. Cognitive function was assessed with the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE).'))
A(('p', 'The validation meta-analysis included five publicly available 16S rRNA gene sequencing datasets: Beijing (PRJNA946900, n=400 AD/HC), Hangzhou (PRJNA496408, n=61), Lishui (PRJNA633959, n=171), Shanghai (PRJNA489760, n=60), and Turkey (PRJNA734525, n=63), totaling 755 AD/HC fecal samples.'))
A(('h2', '2.2 Discovery Cohort: 16S rRNA Sequencing and Fecal Metabolomics'))
A(('p', 'Fecal samples were collected at enrollment. Bacterial 16S rRNA gene sequencing (V3-V4 region) was performed on the Illumina platform, and sequences were processed with QIIME2. Taxonomic classification was performed at the genus level. For co-abundance and network analyses, genera detected in at least 20% of samples (\u226517 of 84) and with mean relative abundance \u22650.01% were retained, yielding 117 of 347 detected genera.'))
A(('p', 'Non-targeted fecal metabolomics profiling was conducted using liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS). Metabolite identification was graded per the Metabolomics Standards Initiative (MSI). Analysis was restricted to B(i)-grade high-confidence endogenous metabolites, excluding non-natural or exogenous substances (n=93), pharmaceutical compounds, known confounds, and metabolites with >50% missing values, retaining 1,873 metabolites.'))
A(('h2', '2.3 Validation Cohorts: Data Acquisition and Processing'))
A(('p', 'Raw sequencing data were downloaded from the NCBI Sequence Read Archive (SRA). All datasets were processed with a unified DADA2 pipeline [1] using cohort-specific truncation lengths optimized for each sequencing region (V3-V4: truncLen=270,220; V4-V5: truncLen=230,220). Taxonomic classification used the SILVA v138.1 reference database [2,28]. ASV (exact sequence variant) tables [27] were aggregated to genus level for cross-cohort comparability.'))
A(('h2', '2.4 Co-Abundance Module Analysis'))
A(('p', 'Spearman rank correlations were computed among the 117 filtered genera across all 84 discovery samples. A distance matrix of (1\u2212\u03c1)/2 was clustered by hierarchical clustering with average linkage, and co-abundance modules were delimited using the maxclust criterion at k=6; scanning cut heights 0.30\u20130.80 yielded no solution in which all modules contained \u22655 genera, and the k=6 cut produced the most interpretable module structure. The module eigengene was computed as the first principal component of the Z-standardized abundances of member genera. Eigengenes were correlated with disease stage (ordinal: HC=0, SCD=1, MCI=2, AD=3) and MMSE score using Spearman correlation; group differences were tested by one-way ANOVA with Tukey HSD post-hoc tests; partial Spearman correlations adjusted for age, sex, and education years were also computed. Enrichment of the 16 curated SCFA-producing genera (Section 2.7) within the protective module was tested by Fisher\u2019s exact test. Module robustness was assessed by re-cutting the dendrogram at k=4\u201310 and recomputing the eigengene\u2013stage correlation for the module containing the core SCFA producers.'))
A(('h2', '2.5 Meta-Analysis'))
A(('p', 'For each validation cohort, differential abundance between AD and HC was assessed using MaAsLin2 [3] with TSS normalization, LOG transformation, and linear models, adjusting for covariates where available. Genus-level effect sizes (coefficients) and standard errors were combined across cohorts using random-effects meta-analysis with the restricted maximum-likelihood (REML) estimator in the metafor R package. Between-study heterogeneity was quantified with I\u00b2 statistics. Multiple testing correction used the Benjamini\u2013Hochberg procedure.'))
A(('p', 'Sensitivity analyses included leave-one-cohort-out analysis and Egger\u2019s test for publication bias. Subgroup analysis was performed by sequencing region (V3-V4 vs. V4-V5). Meta-analysis was conducted using the metafor package [4].'))
A(('h2', '2.6 Machine Learning'))
A(('p', 'Leave-one-cohort-out cross-validation (LOCO-CV) was performed with Lasso-penalized logistic regression (glmnet) [5]. For each fold, four cohorts served as training data and the held-out cohort as test data. Performance was assessed by the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC). Feature importance was extracted from non-zero Lasso coefficients. A secondary analysis used only SCFA-producing genera to evaluate their standalone predictive capacity.'))
A(('h2', '2.7 SCFA-Producing Guild, Hydrogen-Metabolizing Guild, and Functional Pathway Analysis'))
A(('p', 'A curated list of SCFA-producing genera was compiled from published literature, including butyrate producers (Faecalibacterium, Roseburia, Butyricicoccus, Coprococcus, Eubacterium, Anaerostipes, Agathobacter, Lachnospira, Lachnospiraceae members), propionate producers (Bacteroides, Prevotella, Dialister, Veillonella), and acetate producers (Bifidobacterium, Lactobacillus, Streptococcus, Enterococcus). Total SCFA-producer abundance was calculated per sample and compared between AD and HC within each cohort.'))
A(('p', 'For functional pathway inference, genus-level abundance was mapped to KEGG categories using a curated genus-to-pathway mapping based on known metabolic capabilities. Seven pathways were evaluated: butanoate metabolism (map00650), propanoate metabolism (map00640), acetate production, total SCFA production, bile acid metabolism (map00120/00121), tryptophan metabolism (map00380), and LPS biosynthesis (map00540). Pathway abundance was computed as the sum of member genera per sample. Differential pathway abundance between AD and HC was assessed per cohort with Mann-Whitney U tests, using Cohen\u2019s d as effect size. Cross-cohort pooling used random-effects meta-analysis.'))
A(('p', 'Hydrogen-metabolizing guild analysis. In addition to SCFA producers, we defined functional guilds for hydrogen (H\u2082) metabolism based on published literature on microbial hydrogenase pathways. The fermentative H\u2082-producing guild included 40 genera with confirmed [FeFe]- or [NiFe]-hydrogenase pathways (Enterobacteriaceae via formate hydrogen lyase, Clostridium/Eubacterium/Ruminococcus via ferredoxin hydrogenase, Bifidobacterium via bifid shunt, Bacteroides/Prevotella via fermentation). H\u2082-consuming guilds included sulfate-reducing bacteria (Desulfovibrio, Bilophila; dissimilatory sulfate reduction consuming H\u2082 and producing H\u2082S), acetogens (Blautia, Eubacterium hallii group, Acetobacterium; reductive acetyl-CoA pathway), and total H\u2082 consumers. An H\u2082 producer-to-consumer balance index was computed as the ratio of core H\u2082 producer abundance to total H\u2082 consumer abundance. Guild abundances were compared between AD and HC per cohort using Mann-Whitney U tests with Cohen\u2019s d, and pooled via random-effects meta-analysis. The same guild definitions were applied to the discovery cohort: per-sample guild abundance (sum of member-genus relative abundances) was compared across the four diagnostic groups using the Kruskal\u2013Wallis test with post-hoc Mann-Whitney U tests, correlated with disease stage and MMSE (Spearman), and partial Spearman correlations adjusted for age, sex, and education were computed.'))
A(('p', 'PICRUSt2 functional prediction. To independently validate the functional inferences based on genus-to-pathway mapping, we performed Phylogenetic Investigation of Communities by Reconstruction of Unobserved States 2 (PICRUSt2) analysis [46] using the ASV-level abundance table. Representative ASV sequences were placed into a reference phylogenetic tree using EPA-ng, and hidden state prediction was performed using castor to estimate gene family copy numbers for each ASV. Metagenome functional abundances were predicted for KEGG Orthology (KO), Enzyme Commission (EC) numbers, and MetaCyc pathways. The analysis was conducted using the usegalaxy.eu web server with default parameters (reference database: prokaryotic 16S; maximum NSTI cutoff: 2.0). Differential pathway abundance between AD and HC was assessed within each cohort using Wilcoxon rank-sum tests with Hedges\u2019 g effect sizes, followed by random-effects meta-analysis across cohorts with sufficient data (Beijing, Lishui, and Shanghai; n=631; the Turkish cohort did not contribute HC-labeled controls to this comparison). Multiple testing correction was applied using the Benjamini\u2013Hochberg false discovery rate (FDR) method.'))
A(('h2', '2.8 Statistical Analysis'))
A(('p', 'All analyses were performed in R v4.6.1. Alpha diversity indices (Shannon, Simpson, Chao1, observed richness) were computed with the vegan package [6]. Beta diversity was assessed by Bray-Curtis distance and PERMANOVA (999 permutations). Differential metabolite analysis used Wilcoxon rank-sum tests with FDR correction. A p-value <0.05 was considered statistically significant unless otherwise noted.'))
A(('h2', '2.9 Two-Sample Mendelian Randomization'))
A(('p', 'Two-sample Mendelian randomization (MR) was performed using summary-level GWAS data to infer causal relationships between gut microbiota and AD risk [18,43]. Genetic instruments for gut microbiota were derived from the MiBioGen consortium GWAS [40] (Kurilshikov et al. 2021, n=18,340), which reported SNP-microbiota associations for 211 bacterial taxa at the genus level. We focused on 33 genera identified as differentially abundant in our meta-analysis or belonging to the SCFA-producing guild. Instrumental variables were selected at P<1\u00d710\u207b\u2075 and clumped for linkage disequilibrium within 500-kb windows, yielding 370 independent SNPs across 30 genera (3 genera with <3 instruments were excluded).'))
A(('p', 'Outcome data came from the IGAP Stage 1 AD GWAS [41] (Kunkle et al. 2019, n=63,926 cases/controls). SNP effects were harmonized by aligning effect alleles between exposure and outcome datasets; mismatched SNPs were strand-flipped, and palindromic SNPs were resolved using allele frequencies. The primary MR estimate was the inverse-variance weighted (IVW) method. Sensitivity analyses included MR-Egger regression (directional pleiotropy via intercept test), weighted median estimator (robust to invalid instruments), and Cochran\u2019s Q statistic for heterogeneity. Effect sizes are reported as odds ratios (OR) per 1-SD increase in genetically predicted genus abundance.'))
A(('p', 'Bidirectional MR was performed to evaluate reverse causation (AD\u2192microbiota) using the full MiBioGen genus-level summary statistics (131 genera). Genetic instruments for AD were derived from the IGAP GWAS at P<1\u00d710\u207b\u2075 and clumped for LD within 500-kb windows, yielding 45 independent SNPs. These SNPs were queried across all 131 MiBioGen genus files, and SNP effects were harmonized between exposure and outcome (allele alignment and strand-flipping; palindromic SNPs resolved by allele frequency). IVW MR was performed for 105 genera with \u22653 matched instruments. Reverse MR effect sizes represent the change in genus abundance per 1-SE increase in genetically predicted AD liability.'))
A(('h2', '2.10 Microbe\u2013Metabolite Association Network'))
A(('p', 'Spearman rank correlations were computed between all 117 filtered genera and all 1,873 metabolites across the 84 discovery samples. P-values were corrected by the Benjamini\u2013Hochberg FDR. Because this correlation structure was dense (22,658 genus\u2013metabolite pairs at FDR<0.05), a high-confidence network was defined by requiring both FDR<0.05 and |\u03c1|\u22650.6 (167 edges; 21 genera, 86 metabolites), with a threshold gradient (|\u03c1|\u22650.50/0.55/0.65/0.70 \u2192 1,381/509/47/1 edges) used for sensitivity. Hub structure was assessed by degree centrality; its statistical significance was evaluated by permuting genus abundance profiles 2,000 times and recomputing the mean degree of the top three hubs. Enrichment of SCFA producers among hub genera and among network edges was tested by Fisher\u2019s exact test and label permutation, respectively. Module-level microbe\u2013metabolite associations were assessed by correlating the protective-module eigengene with each metabolite (Spearman, Benjamini\u2013Hochberg FDR). The network was visualized with a force-directed layout, with node size proportional to degree and edge color indicating correlation direction.'))
A(('h2', '2.11 Taurine and Mediation Analysis'))
A(('p', 'Fecal taurine (a B(i)-grade metabolite) was compared across diagnostic groups (Kruskal\u2013Wallis test with post-hoc Mann-Whitney U tests), correlated with disease stage and MMSE (Spearman), and correlated with genus abundances (Spearman; Benjamini\u2013Hochberg FDR correction across genera). A rank-based mediation analysis quantified the indirect contribution of fecal taurine to the Blautia\u2013MMSE association: exposure and mediator were rank-transformed, the a-path (Blautia\u2192taurine) and b-path (taurine\u2192MMSE, adjusted for Blautia) were estimated by ordinary least squares, and the indirect effect (a\u00d7b) was tested against 5,000 bootstrap resamples (percentile 95% CI). This analysis is exploratory given the cross-sectional design.'))

A(('h1', '3. Results'))
A(('h2', '3.1 Discovery Cohort: A Protective SCFA-Enriched Co-Abundance Module Declines Across the AD Continuum'))
A(('p', 'We first characterized the co-abundance structure of the discovery cohort (n=84; HC/SCD/MCI/AD), building on our prior characterization of gut microbiota\u2013metabolite alterations across the AD continuum [44,45]. As previously reported [44,45], participants were well-matched for age and sex across diagnostic groups. Hierarchical clustering of the 117 filtered genera yielded six co-abundance modules. The module most strongly associated with disease (module M5) comprised 33 genera, 29 of which (88%) were Firmicutes, including the canonical butyrate producers Faecalibacterium, Roseburia, Blautia, Butyricicoccus, Coprococcus, Agathobacter, Eubacterium hallii group, Subdoligranulum, Lachnospira, Dorea, Fusicatenibacter, and Ruminococcus torques group, alongside several oral-associated commensals (Streptococcus, Veillonella, Rothia, Actinomyces, Granulicatella, Haemophilus). Eleven of the 16 curated SCFA-producing genera mapped to this module, a significant enrichment (Fisher\u2019s exact test, OR=7.9, p=3.3\u00d710\u207b\u2074).'))
A(('p', 'The M5 eigengene was strongly negatively correlated with disease stage (Spearman r=\u22120.687, p=5.2\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u00b3) and positively with MMSE (r=+0.673, p=2.3\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u00b2), and declined monotonically across the continuum (mean eigengene: HC 0.115, SCD 0.072, MCI \u22120.010, AD \u22120.086; one-way ANOVA F=23.1, p=7.1\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u00b9; Figure 2A). Post-hoc testing confirmed significant stepwise differences between all adjacent groups except HC vs. SCD (Tukey: HC\u2013SCD p=0.50; HC\u2013MCI p=0.0007; HC\u2013AD p<0.0001; SCD\u2013MCI p=0.007; SCD\u2013AD p<0.0001; MCI\u2013AD p=0.006). The association survived adjustment for age, sex, and education (partial r=\u22120.455, p=1.4\u00d710\u207b\u2075) and was robust to the module-cut choice (k=4\u201310: r=\u22120.69 to \u22120.71; at k=4\u20135 the SCFA producers merged into a 41-genus module, r=\u22120.71).'))
A(('p', 'A second large module (M2, 64 genera) was positively correlated with disease stage (r=+0.358, p=8.3\u00d710\u207b\u2074) and negatively with MMSE (r=\u22120.409, p=1.3\u00d710\u207b\u2074). M2 aggregated taxa commonly considered pro-inflammatory or bile-acid-tolerant\u2014including Bacteroides, Alistipes, Akkermansia, Bilophila, Desulfovibrio, Escherichia\u2013Shigella, Fusobacterium, Prevotella, and Collinsella\u2014together with Christensenellaceae and Ruminococcaceae lineages that dominate the high-confidence microbe\u2013metabolite network (Section 3.7).'))
A(('p', 'At the guild level, the SCFA-producing guild (16 genera) declined with disease stage (r=\u22120.538, p=1.4\u00d710\u207b\u2077; MMSE r=+0.542, p=9.8\u00d710\u207b\u2078), was significantly depleted in AD versus HC (Mann-Whitney p=1.2\u00d710\u207b\u2074), and remained associated with stage after covariate adjustment (partial r=\u22120.327, p=0.0024). The hydrogen-consuming acetogenic guild (Blautia and Eubacterium hallii group, the principal H\u2082-utilizing butyrate producers) was even more strongly depleted (r=\u22120.575, p=1.1\u00d710\u207b\u2078; partial r=\u22120.410, p=1.1\u00d710\u207b\u2074; AD vs. HC p=1.1\u00d710\u207b\u2075). In contrast, the broad 13-genus fermentative H\u2082-producing guild showed only a non-significant trend in this cohort (r=\u22120.142, p=0.20), while the H\u2082S-producing guild (Desulfovibrio, Bilophila, Fusobacterium) showed a nominal increase (r=+0.223, p=0.042) that did not survive covariate adjustment (p=0.84) and was directionally discordant with the validation meta-analysis (Section 3.3), consistent with cohort-specific behavior of these low-abundance taxa.'))
A(('p', 'At single-genus resolution, Blautia showed the most marked depletion, with a median relative abundance of 0.28% in HC versus 0.05% in AD (Mann-Whitney p<10\u207b\u2074; stage r=\u22120.571, p=1.5\u00d710\u207b\u2078; MMSE r=+0.538, p=1.3\u00d710\u207b\u2077). Butyricicoccus (p=0.003) and Coprococcus (p=0.010) were also significantly depleted in AD versus HC, and Faecalibacterium (r=\u22120.269, p=0.013) and Roseburia (r=\u22120.267, p=0.014) declined significantly with disease stage, although their AD-versus-HC differences did not reach significance in this modest sample (p=0.39 and p=0.10, respectively). Conversely, Akkermansia was increased in AD (median relative abundance 0.00% in HC vs. 0.003% in AD, p=0.025; stage r=+0.323, p=0.003).'))
A(('h2', '3.2 Validation: Multi-Cohort Meta-Analysis Confirms Depleted Butyrate Producers'))
A(('p', 'We validated these findings in five independent cohorts totaling 755 AD/HC fecal samples. Cohort and demographic characteristics are summarized in Supplementary Table S1.'))
A(('p', 'Alpha diversity: Meta-analysis across five cohorts showed no significant difference between AD and HC (Shannon: Hedges\u2019 g=\u22120.187, 95% CI [\u22120.472, 0.099], p=0.200; richness: g=\u22120.041, p=0.759; Chao1: g=\u22120.039, p=0.767), though the Shannon index showed a downward trend in three of five cohorts. Per-index alpha-diversity meta-analytic statistics are provided in Supplementary Table S5.'))
A(('p', 'Beta diversity: PERMANOVA revealed significant separation between AD and HC in 3/5 cohorts (Lishui: R\u00b2=0.061, p=0.001; Beijing: R\u00b2=0.019, p=0.001; Shanghai: R\u00b2=0.172, p=0.001), with marginal significance in Hangzhou (p=0.063) and Turkey (p=0.090). Full per-cohort PERMANOVA results are provided in Supplementary Table S6.'))
A(('p', 'Differential abundance: Random-effects meta-analysis identified 24 genera with FDR<0.05 (10 enriched in AD, 14 depleted). Key findings are summarized in Table 1; complete per-genus statistics for all 24 genera are provided in Supplementary Table S2, and the per-cohort effect-size profile is shown in Supplementary Figure S5.'))
A(('p', 'AD-enriched genera included Lactobacillus (pooled coef=+0.529, p=1.46e-7, I\u00b2=13.2%), Incertae Sedis (coef=+0.434, p=1.80e-7, I\u00b2=0%), Collinsella (coef=+0.508, p=8.44e-6, I\u00b2=6.5%), Ruminococcus gnavus group (coef=+0.566, p=1.56e-4, I\u00b2=44.8%), Enterococcus (coef=+0.403, p=3.98e-4, I\u00b2=46.4%). Akkermansia showed a nominal enrichment (coef=+0.549, p=0.029, I\u00b2=71.6%) that did not survive FDR correction (q=0.110).'))
A(('p', 'AD-depleted genera included Eubacterium eligens group (coef=\u22120.355, p=6.48e-7, I\u00b2=0%), Romboutsia (coef=\u22120.617, p=3.75e-6, I\u00b2=21.3%), Dialister (coef=\u22120.796, p=4.37e-5, I\u00b2=50.6%), Lachnospiraceae NK4A136 group (coef=\u22120.329, p=1.17e-4, I\u00b2=0%), Butyricicoccus (coef=\u22120.457, p=0.007, I\u00b2=54.7%), Agathobacter (coef=\u22120.617, p=0.005, I\u00b2=68.6%), Lachnospira (coef=\u22120.344, p=0.008, I\u00b2=72.9%), Clostridium sensu stricto 1 (coef=\u22120.404, p=0.011, I\u00b2=38.9%), and Coprococcus (coef=\u22120.357, nominal p=0.045, FDR=0.144, I\u00b2=72.2%).'))
A(('p', 'Multiple depleted genera are well-characterized butyrate producers (Butyricicoccus, Coprococcus, Eubacterium, Lachnospiraceae members), directly validating the discovery cohort\u2019s protective module.'))
A(('p', 'Sensitivity analysis: Leave-one-cohort-out analysis confirmed that 21/24 significant genera maintained consistent directionality across all subsets. Egger\u2019s test indicated minimal publication bias (only 6/90 genera with p<0.05). Funnel plots for key taxa are shown in Supplementary Figure S1.'))
A(('p', 'Subgroup analysis by sequencing region: Cohorts were stratified into V3-V4 (Hangzhou, Shanghai, Lishui, Turkey; n=355) and V4-V5 (Beijing; n=400) subgroups to assess whether technical heterogeneity in 16S rRNA variable regions influenced the results. Directional consistency was high: all core butyrate-producing genera showed consistent depletion in AD across both subgroups. In the V3-V4 subgroup, significant depletion was observed for Agathobacter (coef=\u22120.771, p=0.014), Lachnospira (coef=\u22120.507, p=3.5e-4), Butyricicoccus (coef=\u22120.485, p=0.046), Romboutsia (coef=\u22120.442, p=0.006), and Lachnospiraceae NK4A136 group (coef=\u22120.349, p=0.002). In the V4-V5 subgroup, significant depletion was observed for Faecalibacterium (coef=\u22120.842, p=5.5e-5), Coprococcus (coef=\u22120.776, p=2.3e-5), Butyricicoccus (coef=\u22120.619, p=1.7e-4), Roseburia (coef=\u22120.342, p=0.029), and Blautia (coef=\u22120.461, p=0.002). Faecalibacterium and Roseburia reached significance only in the V4-V5 Beijing cohort, suggesting that the overall meta-analytic trend for these taxa may be partly driven by the Beijing cohort. The high directional concordance across sequencing regions validates the robustness of the core findings (Supplementary Figure S2).'))
A(('h2', '3.3 SCFA-Producing Guild Analysis'))
A(('p', 'Dedicated analysis of SCFA-producing genera revealed consistent depletion of butyrate producers in AD: Butyricicoccus (coef=\u22120.457, p=0.007, FDR=0.037), Coprococcus (coef=\u22120.357, p=0.045, FDR=0.144), Faecalibacterium (coef=\u22120.557, p=0.189, trend, I\u00b2=93.2%), and Roseburia (coef=\u22120.303, p=0.112, trend, I\u00b2=74.5%).'))
A(('p', 'Propionate producer Dialister was significantly depleted (coef=\u22120.796, p=4.37e-5). Conversely, acetate producers Lactobacillus (coef=+0.529, p=1.46e-7) and Bifidobacterium (coef=+0.577, p=0.017) were enriched in AD.'))
A(('p', 'Total SCFA-producer abundance was significantly lower in AD in the Shanghai cohort (log2FC=\u22120.707, p=1.09e-4) and showed a downward trend in Beijing (log2FC=\u22120.138, p=0.138) and Hangzhou (log2FC=\u22120.380, p=0.061).'))
A(('p', 'Beyond SCFA production, many butyrate-producing Firmicutes also generate molecular hydrogen (H\u2082) via fermentation, a gaseous metabolite with established antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, and neuroprotective properties. Fermentative H\u2082-producing guild was significantly depleted in AD (pooled d=\u22120.186, p=0.016, I\u00b2=0%), with consistent direction across all four cohorts with sufficient data. H\u2082S-producing guild was also significantly depleted (d=\u22120.299, p=0.0015). In contrast, sulfate-reducing H\u2082-consuming bacteria did not change significantly (d=+0.075, p=0.634), indicating that the deficit is specific to H\u2082-producing rather than H\u2082-consuming guilds. The guild-level forest plot is provided in Supplementary Figure S3.'))
A(('h2', '3.4 Functional Pathway Inference Confirms Depleted SCFA Biosynthetic Capacity'))
A(('p', 'Genus-level abundance was mapped to KEGG metabolic pathways using a curated genus-to-pathway dictionary. Random-effects meta-analysis across four cohorts with sufficient data showed that total SCFA-producing capacity was significantly depleted in AD (pooled d=\u22120.524, p=0.004, I\u00b2=75.0%).'))
A(('p', 'At the individual pathway level, butanoate metabolism (KEGG map00650) showed a strong downward trend (pooled d=\u22120.502, p=0.080, I\u00b2=89.8%), with significant depletion in Beijing (d=\u22120.743, p=1.9e-12), Lishui (d=\u22121.154, p=2.2e-12), and Hangzhou (d=\u22120.492, p=0.039). Propanoate metabolism (map00640) also trended toward depletion (pooled d=\u22120.639, p=0.052, I\u00b2=92.2%), with highly significant reductions in Beijing (d=\u22120.631, p=5.5e-12) and Shanghai (d=\u22121.977, p=4.6e-9). Bile acid metabolism, tryptophan metabolism, and LPS biosynthesis pathways did not reach statistical significance in pooled analysis, though directionality was consistently negative for all pathways except acetate producers. Pooled and per-cohort pathway meta-analysis results are provided in Supplementary Table S4.'))
A(('p', 'These functional inferences support the taxonomic findings: not only are butyrate-producing genera depleted in AD, but their aggregate biosynthetic capacity for SCFA production is functionally diminished.'))
A(('p', 'Independent validation by PICRUSt2. To confirm these functional inferences with an unbiased computational approach, we performed PICRUSt2 metagenome prediction at the ASV level across three validation cohorts with sufficient AD-HC pairs (Beijing, Lishui, Shanghai; n=631). Random-effects meta-analysis of 309 MetaCyc pathways identified 38 pathways with nominal significance (p<0.05) and 11 with FDR<0.05. Consistent with the genus-to-pathway mapping, SCFA biosynthetic pathways were among the most significantly depleted: acetate production via the acetyl-CoA pathway (METH-ACETATE-PWY, d=\u22120.413, FDR=1.4\u00d710\u207b\u2074), propionate fermentation (PWY-5022, d=\u22120.317, FDR=0.048); butyrate fermentation (PWY-5676, d=\u22120.168) showed a concordant nominal reduction (p=0.048) that did not survive FDR correction (FDR=0.392) and was estimable in only two of three cohorts, and should therefore be considered supportive rather than confirmatory. Top 20 differentially abundant pathways are shown in Supplementary Figure S6, SCFA-related pathways in Supplementary Figure S7, and cohort-specific effect sizes in Supplementary Figure S8.'))
A(('h2', '3.5 Cross-Cohort Machine Learning Validates SCFA-Producing Signatures'))
A(('p', 'LOCO-CV with Lasso logistic regression showed moderate cross-cohort predictive performance, with a mean AUC of 0.656 across five cohorts: Lishui (0.697), Beijing (0.681), Turkey (0.665), Shanghai (0.622), and Hangzhou (0.613). The top discriminative features were Lactobacillus (mean Lasso coefficient +0.276, non-zero in 5/5 held-out cohorts), Methanobrevibacter (+0.231, 4/5), Dialister (\u22120.213, 5/5), Oscillibacter (+0.209, 5/5), and Romboutsia (\u22120.186, 5/5). Cohort-by-cohort LOCO-CV results, including the SCFA-only versus all-genera comparison, are provided in Supplementary Table S3.'))
A(('p', 'Using only SCFA-producing genera as features achieved higher AUC than all genera combined in two cohorts: Lishui (SCFA-only 0.767 vs. all-genera 0.697) and Shanghai (0.698 vs. 0.622). This indicates that SCFA-producing guilds capture the core disease-associated signal, and the full feature set introduces noise that dilutes predictive performance in some cohorts.'))
A(('h2', '3.6 Mendelian Randomization Supports Causal Involvement of Specific Taxa'))
A(('p', 'Two-sample MR was conducted using MiBioGen microbiota GWAS (n=18,340) as exposure and IGAP AD GWAS (n=63,926) as outcome. Of 33 target genera, 30 had \u22653 valid instrumental variables (370 independent SNPs total; 3 genera excluded for insufficient instruments).'))
A(('p', 'The primary IVW analysis identified Bifidobacterium as the only genus with a statistically significant causal association: a 1-SD genetically predicted increase in Bifidobacterium abundance was associated with 30.6% higher AD risk (OR=1.306, 95% CI 1.097\u20131.555, p=0.0027). The weighted median estimator gave a concordant estimate (OR=1.300, p<0.001), and Cochran\u2019s Q statistic indicated no detectable heterogeneity (Q p=0.941, I\u00b2=0%). Leave-one-SNP-out analyses confirmed that the association was not driven by any single instrument, with odds ratios remaining above 1.27 and significant after removal of each of the three SNPs (all p<0.04). MR-Egger yielded a point estimate in the opposite direction (OR=0.507, p=0.227; intercept p=0.092), but with only three instrumental variables this estimate is statistically unstable (two residual degrees of freedom) and its wide confidence interval overlaps the IVW estimate; it should therefore not be interpreted as evidence against the IVW result. MR-PRESSO could not be reliably applied because the Bifidobacterium instrument set comprised only three SNPs, fewer than the minimum number for which its global and distortion tests are valid. These sensitivity analyses are summarized in Supplementary Figure S4.'))
A(('p', 'Lachnospiraceae UCG010 showed a suggestive protective trend by IVW (OR=0.885, 95% CI 0.772\u20131.015, p=0.082), supported by the weighted median (OR=0.875, p=0.003) and MR-Egger (p=0.011) estimators, with no detectable heterogeneity (Cochran\u2019s Q p=0.959). In contrast, Dialister showed no causal association (IVW OR=0.965, 95% CI 0.839\u20131.109, p=0.615), with concordant null estimates from the weighted median and MR-Egger and no directionally consistent leave-one-SNP-out result.'))
A(('p', 'The major butyrate-producing genera\u2014Roseburia, Faecalibacterium, Coprococcus, and Butyricicoccus\u2014did not reach statistical significance in IVW analyses (all p>0.19), although effect directions were generally consistent with a protective role. This suggests that while observational data strongly support butyrate producer depletion in AD, the genetic evidence for causal involvement of individual genera may be limited by low heritability or insufficient statistical power for these taxa.'))
A(('p', 'Bidirectional MR was performed using the full MiBioGen genus-level summary statistics (131 genera) to examine reverse causation, with SNP effects harmonized between exposure and outcome. Using 45 LD-independent AD risk SNPs (P<1\u00d710\u207b\u2075) as instruments, reverse MR (AD\u2192microbiota) was performed for 105 genera with \u22653 matched instruments. Thirteen genera showed nominal reverse associations (p<0.05), of which one survived FDR correction: genetically predicted AD liability was significantly associated with lower Bacteroides abundance (IVW \u03b2=\u22120.039, p=1.35\u00d710\u207b\u2074, FDR=0.014), suggesting that disease processes may themselves reduce Bacteroides carriage. Nominal reverse associations included Ruminococcaceae UCG013 (\u03b2=\u22120.034, p=0.0013), Senegalimassilia (\u03b2=+0.051, p=0.0017), Flavonifractor (p=0.007), Oscillibacter (p=0.007), and Holdemanella (p=0.008), none of which survived FDR correction. Critically, Bifidobacterium showed no reverse association (p=0.626), providing evidence against reverse causation for the key forward MR signal.'))
A(('h2', '3.7 Microbe\u2013Metabolite Network Architecture and Fecal Taurine Depletion'))
A(('p', 'We next integrated the paired genus and metabolite profiles of the discovery cohort (117 genera \u00d7 1,873 metabolites, n=84), extending our prior integrative analysis [45]. The raw correlation structure was dense (22,658 genus\u2013metabolite pairs at FDR<0.05); we therefore defined a high-confidence network requiring both FDR<0.05 and |\u03c1|\u22650.6, which comprised 167 edges (64 positive, 103 negative) across 21 genera and 86 metabolites (Figure 9).'))
A(('p', 'The dominant hubs were Christensenellaceae R-7 group (47 edges), UCG-005 (26), Family XIII AD3011 group (16), Oscillospiraceae (15), Ruminococcaceae (13), and Oscillospirales (13); this hub structure was robust at the adjacent threshold |\u03c1|\u22650.55 (509 edges; same leading hubs with 87, 51, and 52 edges for the top three). The observed mean degree of the top three hubs (29.7) greatly exceeded the permutation null (0.0; 2,000 permutations, p<0.0005), demonstrating that hub architecture is far stronger than expected by chance.'))
A(('p', 'Notably, the hub genera were members of the risk-associated module M2 rather than the protective module: M5 genera averaged only 1.0 strong-edge connections versus 9.1 for non-M5 genera. Several hubs increased with disease stage (Family XIII AD3011 group r=+0.254, p=0.020; Oscillospiraceae r=+0.259, p=0.017; Ruminococcaceae r=+0.246, p=0.024), whereas Christensenellaceae R-7 group and UCG-005 showed no significant stage association. SCFA-producing genera were not enriched among hubs (Fisher\u2019s exact test p=0.60) nor in their share of network edges (permutation p=0.54). Thus, in this cohort the SCFA signal is organized as a coordinated, moderately-connected protective module rather than as individual high-degree hubs, while the metabolically most influential hub positions are occupied by risk-module Firmicutes lineages.'))
A(('p', 'At the module level, the M5 eigengene was associated with 704 metabolites at FDR<0.05 (Spearman \u03c1 up to +0.639). The strongest positive associations included nucleoside metabolites\u20145-methyluridine (\u03c1=0.639), uridine (0.622), 5-methylcytosine (0.616), and inosine (0.571)\u2014the vitamin B3 metabolite nicotinic acid (0.628), the tryptophan-pathway metabolite picolinic acid (0.596), and the prostaglandin catabolite 2,3-dinor prostaglandin E1 (0.638); the strongest negative associations included N-cyclohexanecarbonyltetradecylamine (\u22120.590), the dipeptide Lys-Leu-Gln (\u22120.492), and sulfocholic acid (\u22120.456). Taurine was positively associated with the M5 eigengene (\u03c1=0.255, p=0.019, FDR=0.050).'))
A(('p', 'Taurine showed a significant group-wise decline (Kruskal\u2013Wallis p=0.022), with median normalized intensities of 6.46 (HC), 5.28 (SCD), 5.56 (MCI), and 4.87 (AD); AD was significantly lower than HC (Mann-Whitney p=0.006). Fecal taurine was negatively correlated with disease stage (r=\u22120.324, p=0.003) and positively with MMSE (r=+0.360, p=7.6\u00d710\u207b\u2074). Across genera, 36 taxa were significantly associated with taurine at FDR<0.05. The strongest negative associations were Oscillospirales (\u22120.496), Alistipes (\u22120.494), Oscillospiraceae (\u22120.484), Christensenella (\u22120.461), NK4A214 group (\u22120.453), Intestinimonas (\u22120.446), and Christensenellaceae R-7 group (\u22120.431)\u2014the same risk-module lineages that dominate the high-confidence network hubs. The strongest positive association was Ruminococcus gnavus group (+0.373, FDR=0.003), followed by Dorea (+0.232) and Blautia (+0.216, nominal p=0.048).'))
A(('p', 'Because Blautia was the single most depleted protective genus and was positively associated with taurine, we performed an exploratory rank-based mediation analysis of the Blautia\u2013MMSE association (total effect c=0.161). The a-path (Blautia\u2192taurine) was 0.214 and the b-path (taurine\u2192MMSE, adjusted for Blautia) was 0.146, yielding an indirect effect of 0.031 (19.5% of the total effect) and a direct effect of 0.067. The bootstrap 95% CI of the indirect effect (0.0003\u20130.0679) excluded zero, nominally supporting partial mediation; given the cross-sectional design and the lower CI bound close to zero, this result should be interpreted as hypothesis-generating.'))

A(('h1', '4. Discussion'))
A(('p', 'This integrative study combines multi-omics network analysis in a discovery cohort with large-scale meta-analysis across five independent validation cohorts, providing convergent evidence for progressive erosion of butyrate-producing microbial consortia\u2014and depletion of the neuroactive metabolite taurine\u2014along the AD continuum.'))
A(('h2', '4.1 Convergent Evidence for Depleted Butyrate Producers'))
A(('p', 'The principal finding\u2014depletion of SCFA-producing Firmicutes in AD\u2014was identified through two complementary approaches and validated across multiple independent cohorts. In the discovery cohort, co-abundance analysis identified a protective module dominated by butyrate and propionate producers (Faecalibacterium, Roseburia, Blautia, Butyricicoccus, Coprococcus, Agathobacter, Eubacterium hallii group) that declined progressively and strongly from SCD through MCI to AD (r=\u22120.687, p=5.2\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u00b3; adjusted for age, sex, and education, r=\u22120.455). In the validation meta-analysis, 14 of 24 differentially abundant genera were depleted in AD, including multiple well-characterized butyrate producers (Butyricicoccus, Coprococcus, Eubacterium, Lachnospiraceae members).'))
A(('p', 'Butyrate serves as the primary energy source for colonic epithelial cells, maintains intestinal barrier integrity, attenuates systemic inflammation, and can traverse the blood\u2013brain barrier to exert direct neuroprotective effects via microglial modulation [10,25,31,32]. Preclinical studies have shown that butyrate supplementation reduces amyloid burden, tau phosphorylation, and cognitive impairment in AD mouse models [25,31]. The consistent depletion of butyrate producers across the AD continuum therefore has direct mechanistic implications.'))
A(('p', 'Our functional pathway inference extends these taxonomic findings by showing that aggregate SCFA biosynthetic capacity\u2014rather than merely individual taxa\u2014is significantly diminished in AD (pooled d=\u22120.524, p=0.004). The coordinated downregulation of both butanoate and propanoate metabolism pathways suggests a systemic loss of SCFA production capacity, not just shifts in individual taxa.'))
A(('p', 'Beyond SCFAs, our hydrogen-metabolizing guild analysis reveals a previously unrecognized dimension of AD-associated dysbiosis. In the validation meta-analysis, fermentative H\u2082-producing bacteria were significantly depleted (pooled d=\u22120.186, p=0.016, I\u00b2=0%). In the discovery cohort, the most strongly depleted hydrogen-metabolizing guild was the H\u2082-consuming acetogenic consortium (Blautia and Eubacterium hallii group; r=\u22120.575), which converts H\u2082 and CO\u2082 into acetate via the Wood\u2013Ljungdahl pathway and is functionally coupled to butyrate production. The concordant depletion of H\u2082 producers across validation cohorts and of acetogenic H\u2082 consumers in the discovery cohort suggests that both limbs of hydrogen turnover are compromised in AD. Molecular hydrogen is a selective antioxidant that scavenges cytotoxic hydroxyl radicals and peroxynitrite [54], and the concurrent depletion of H\u2082 and H\u2082S producers in the validation cohorts suggests a dual energetic deficit, extending the SCFA framework to include gaseous metabolites.'))
A(('p', 'These functional inferences based on genus-to-pathway mapping were independently corroborated by PICRUSt2 metagenome prediction at the ASV level across three validation cohorts (n=631). Random-effects meta-analysis of 309 MetaCyc pathways revealed consistent depletion of SCFA biosynthetic pathways, including acetate production (METH-ACETATE-PWY, FDR=1.4\u00d710\u207b\u2074), propionate fermentation (PWY-5022, FDR=0.048), with a concordant but nominal (non-FDR-significant) reduction in butyrate fermentation (PWY-5676, nominal p=0.048). The convergence of two independent functional inference approaches\u2014curated genus-to-pathway mapping and ASV-level PICRUSt2 prediction\u2014substantially strengthens the functional interpretation of the taxonomic findings.'))
A(('h2', '4.2 Comparison with Prior Meta-Analyses'))
A(('p', 'Our findings align with and extend several recent meta-analyses of gut microbiota in AD. The most comprehensive prior quantitative meta-analysis [14,15,16,29] (13 studies, 581 AD-spectrum patients and 445 healthy controls) reported consistent depletion of six SCFA-producing genera\u2014Ruminococcus (SMD=\u22120.45), Faecalibacterium (SMD=\u22120.38), Coprococcus (SMD=\u22120.34), Roseburia (SMD=\u22120.31), Butyricicoccus (SMD=\u22120.29), and Eubacterium (SMD=\u22120.27)\u2014with enrichment of Bifidobacterium (SMD=+0.33) and Lactobacillus (SMD=+0.41). Our results are directionally consistent with these findings and extend them by demonstrating progressive depletion across the AD continuum, functional pathway validation, and causal inference.'))
A(('p', 'Unlike prior meta-analyses that pooled published relative-abundance summaries generated by heterogeneous bioinformatic pipelines, our study reprocessed all raw sequencing data through a standardized DADA2 workflow with SILVA classification, minimizing technical batch effects\u2014a critical advantage given the known sensitivity of 16S rRNA results to bioinformatic choices.'))
A(('p', 'Prior meta-analyses have reported regional heterogeneity for Bacteroides, Bifidobacterium, and Alistipes, with Bifidobacterium showing inconsistent directions across studies [42]. Our MR analysis provides a perspective on this inconsistency: while observational studies variably report Bifidobacterium enrichment or depletion, the genetic evidence supports a causal risk role for Bifidobacterium (OR=1.306), suggesting that the observational heterogeneity may reflect confounding or reverse causation in individual studies.'))
A(('p', 'A recent bidirectional MR preprint using a smaller microbiome GWAS (N=3,890) reported suggestive associations for Dialister, Parabacteroides, and Ruminococcus, but sensitivity analyses including colocalization indicated that these signals were likely driven by genetic confounding or reverse causation [43]. Our use of the larger MiBioGen GWAS (n=18,340) and full summary statistics with allele harmonization provides more robust causal inference, and our finding of Bifidobacterium as a significant causal risk factor differs from prior smaller MR studies, likely due to increased statistical power.'))
A(('h2', '4.3 Cross-Cohort Generalizability and Machine Learning'))
A(('p', 'The LOCO-CV results (mean AUC=0.656) indicate that gut microbial features have moderate but meaningful cross-cohort predictive capacity for AD status. SCFA-producing genera alone outperformed the full feature set in two cohorts, suggesting that these functional guilds capture the core disease-associated signal, and that including all genera introduces noise that dilutes performance.'))
A(('p', 'The moderate AUC values are consistent with the multifactorial nature of AD, where gut dysbiosis represents one contributing factor among many (aging, genetics, vascular risk, lifestyle). The heterogeneity across cohorts (AUC range 0.613\u20130.697) likely reflects differences in sample size, sequencing depth, diet, medication use, and disease severity. These results suggest that gut microbial features may be most useful as part of a multi-modal biomarker panel rather than as standalone diagnostic tools.'))
A(('h2', '4.4 Integration with the Discovery Cohort: Module Architecture, Hub Taxa, and Taurine'))
A(('p', 'The integration of discovery and validation designs strengthens our conclusions in several ways. The multi-omics discovery cohort provides mechanistic context by linking microbial taxa to specific metabolites and identifying coordinated modules rather than isolated markers. The five-cohort meta-analysis establishes the robustness and generalizability of the findings across diverse populations and sequencing protocols. The MR analysis adds a causal-inference layer that observational studies alone cannot provide.'))
A(('p', 'The microbe\u2013metabolite integration refines the mechanistic interpretation in an important way. In the discovery cohort, SCFA-producing taxa were not the highest-degree hubs of the high-confidence network; instead, the protective signal is organized at the module level\u2014an 88% Firmicutes, SCFA-enriched consortium whose eigengene tracks disease stage (r=\u22120.687) and MMSE (r=+0.673) far more strongly than any single genus. The high-confidence hubs (|\u03c1|\u22650.6 and FDR<0.05) were members of the risk-associated module M2\u2014Christensenellaceae R-7 group, UCG-005, Family XIII AD3011 group, Oscillospiraceae, and Ruminococcaceae lineages\u2014several of which expand with disease stage (r=+0.25 to +0.26) and are negatively correlated with fecal taurine (\u03c1=\u22120.43 to \u22120.50). This architecture indicates that AD-associated dysbiosis simultaneously erodes a coordinated protective module and expands metabolically influential, taurine-negative hub taxa, disrupting bile acid, amino acid, and lipid turnover rather than merely shifting isolated biomarkers.'))
A(('p', 'A particularly compelling mechanistic link emerges from fecal taurine. Taurine was significantly depleted in AD (p=0.006), correlated with MMSE (r=+0.360, p=7.6\u00d710\u207b\u2074), and positively associated with the protective-module eigengene (\u03c1=0.255). Rank-based mediation analysis estimated that fecal taurine accounts for 19.5% of the Blautia\u2013MMSE association (bootstrap 95% CI 0.0003\u20130.0679, excluding zero); given the cross-sectional design and the lower CI bound close to zero, this estimate should be considered exploratory. Taurine is a sulfur-containing amino acid with well-documented neuroactive properties, acting as a GABA_A receptor agonist, osmolyte, antioxidant, and neuromodulator [47,48]. Cerebrospinal fluid taurine depletion has been reported in AD patients since 1991 [49], and dietary taurine supplementation has shown protective effects against multiple aspects of AD pathology in APP/PS1 mouse models [50], with recent 2026 confirmation [51] and a landmark 2023 Science study identifying taurine deficiency as a driver of aging across multiple species [52].'))
A(('p', 'Mechanistically, the link between gut microbiota and fecal taurine likely operates through bile acid metabolism: primary bile acids are conjugated with taurine (or glycine) in the liver before secretion into the gut, and gut bacteria expressing bile salt hydrolase (BSH) deconjugate these bile acids, releasing taurine [53]. The taurine-negative hub taxa identified here include several bile-acid-tolerant lineages (Christensenellaceae, Alistipes, Bacteroides), whereas Blautia, an acetogenic butyrate producer, was positively associated with taurine. The convergent depletion of butyrate-producing consortia may therefore reduce BSH activity and impair taurine release from conjugated bile acids, contributing to the reduced fecal taurine observed in AD. It is noteworthy that our PICRUSt2 functional prediction detected key taurine metabolism orthologs (ko00430), including cysteine dioxygenase (K03782), cysteine sulfinic acid decarboxylase (K01697), and taurine\u2013pyruvate aminotransferase (K11181), indicating that gut microbial communities possess the genetic potential for taurine metabolism. However, because PICRUSt2\u2019s default MetaCyc pathway annotation does not include a dedicated taurine metabolism pathway, we could not directly validate differential abundance of this pathway at the meta-analysis level. This represents an important limitation that warrants targeted metabolomic and metagenomic validation in future studies.'))
A(('p', 'A further observation warranting note is the increase of Akkermansia in AD in both the discovery cohort (stage r=+0.323, p=0.003) and the validation meta-analysis (nominal enrichment, coef=+0.549, I\u00b2=71.6%). This direction is concordant with the consistent Akkermansia enrichment reported in Parkinson\u2019s disease (Section 4.7) and cautions against a blanket \u2018beneficial\u2019 interpretation of mucin-degrading taxa across neurodegenerative diseases.'))
A(('h2', '4.5 Mendelian Randomization and Causal Inference'))
A(('p', 'Our MR analysis provides a causal-inference layer to the gut microbiota\u2013AD literature. The finding that genetically predicted Bifidobacterium abundance is associated with increased AD risk (OR=1.306, p=0.0027) is noteworthy. While Bifidobacterium is widely regarded as a beneficial probiotic and is often enriched in AD patients in observational studies, the causal direction suggested by MR is that higher Bifidobacterium may contribute to AD risk rather than being a consequence of the disease. This finding underscores the complexity of host\u2013microbe interactions and cautions against simplistic \u2018beneficial\u2019 or \u2018harmful\u2019 classifications of gut bacteria.'))
A(('p', 'The suggestive protective association of Lachnospiraceae UCG010 (OR=0.885), supported by both MR-Egger and weighted median methods, aligns with the broader Lachnospiraceae family\u2019s role in butyrate production and intestinal homeostasis. The lack of significant IVW associations for canonical butyrate producers (Roseburia, Faecalibacterium, Coprococcus) may reflect limited statistical power for these taxa in the MiBioGen GWAS, rather than absence of causal effects. Larger microbiome GWAS with improved taxonomic resolution will be needed to definitively assess the causal role of butyrate-producing genera.'))
A(('p', 'Our allele-harmonized bidirectional MR analysis provides evidence against reverse causation for the key forward signal: Bifidobacterium showed no significant reverse association (AD\u2192Bifidobacterium, p=0.626), confirming that the forward signal reflects a genuine Bifidobacterium\u2192AD direction rather than disease-driven changes in gut microbiota. At the same time, the significant reverse association for Bacteroides (\u03b2=\u22120.039, FDR=0.014) indicates that AD liability may itself reduce Bacteroides carriage, suggesting that part of the well-documented observational heterogeneity for Bacteroides [42] may reflect disease-driven remodeling rather than a causal contribution to disease risk.'))
A(('h2', '4.6 Clinical and Translational Implications'))
A(('p', 'The convergent depletion of butyrate-producing consortia across the AD continuum, supported by both observational meta-analysis and causal inference, has direct implications for therapeutic development [37]. Butyrate exerts multifaceted neuroprotective effects\u2014including histone deacetylase inhibition, microglial modulation, blood\u2013brain barrier integrity maintenance, and anti-inflammatory signaling [10,23,31,32]\u2014suggesting that restoration of butyrate production may represent a viable intervention strategy. Several translational pathways warrant consideration: probiotic supplementation with defined butyrate-producing strains (e.g., Faecalibacterium prausnitzii, Roseburia intestinalis, Eubacterium rectale) or next-generation probiotic consortia; prebiotic interventions targeting butyrate producers through dietary fiber (resistant starch, inulin, fructooligosaccharides); postbiotic administration of butyrate or its derivatives (e.g., sodium butyrate, tributyrin) to bypass microbial production; and fecal microbiota transplantation from healthy donors to restore ecological diversity.'))
A(('p', 'The taurine and hydrogen findings extend these translational considerations beyond SCFAs. Oral taurine supplementation is inexpensive, well tolerated, and has shown protection against AD pathology in mouse models [50,51] and against aging-related decline across species [52]; our mediation analysis nominates the Blautia\u2013taurine axis as a plausible microbial route by which such supplementation could act. Likewise, interventions that restore hydrogen turnover\u2014H\u2082-producing or acetogen-supporting probiotics and prebiotics, or molecular hydrogen itself, a selective antioxidant [54]\u2014represent testable strategies for the AD continuum. Because the protective module already declines from SCD onward, the preclinical and MCI stages may represent the critical window for microbiome-targeted interventions, before irreversible neurodegeneration occurs. Clinical trials of microbiome interventions in AD are emerging [26,35,39], including neuromodulation approaches targeting the parasympathetic nervous system to modulate gut\u2013brain communication, and our findings provide a mechanistic rationale and candidate taxa (Blautia, Eubacterium hallii group, Faecalibacterium, Roseburia) for such trials.'))
A(('p', 'The identification of Bifidobacterium as a putative causal risk factor (OR=1.306) adds a cautionary note: not all \u2018beneficial\u2019 bacteria are uniformly protective, and probiotic interventions should be strain-specific and evidence-based rather than broadly applied. The LOCO-CV AUC of 0.656 for gut microbial features suggests that microbial signatures may have utility as adjunctive biomarkers, particularly when combined with other modalities (CSF biomarkers, imaging, genetics).'))
A(('h2', '4.7 Parallels and Distinctions with Parkinson\u2019s Disease'))
A(('p', 'Gut microbial dysbiosis is increasingly recognized as a shared feature of neurodegenerative diseases, and comparison with Parkinson\u2019s disease (PD)\u2014the most extensively studied neurodegenerative condition in the microbiome field\u2014provides valuable context. Both AD and PD exhibit reduced microbial diversity, depletion of butyrate-producing genera (Faecalibacterium, Roseburia, Coprococcus), and enrichment of pro-inflammatory taxa. However, PD shows more consistent enrichment of Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium, whereas AD shows more variable Bifidobacterium directions. Our discovery-cohort finding of increased Akkermansia in AD (r=+0.323, p=0.003) narrows this distinction and aligns AD more closely with PD in this respect. The MR finding of Bifidobacterium as a causal risk factor in AD may partially explain the observational inconsistency. The shared depletion of butyrate producers across both diseases suggests that butyrate deficiency may represent a common final pathway in neurodegeneration, while disease-specific taxa may reflect distinct pathophysiological mechanisms.'))
A(('h2', '4.8 Future Directions'))
A(('p', 'Several avenues warrant further investigation. Longitudinal cohort studies with serial sampling across the AD continuum are essential to establish the temporal dynamics of microbial alterations and determine whether specific taxa predict cognitive decline trajectories. Strain-level resolution through shotgun metagenomics will enable identification of functionally distinct strains within genera that show aggregate-level associations, particularly for Bifidobacterium where strain-specific effects may explain the MR paradox. Mechanistic validation in germ-free or humanized mouse models, particularly fecal transplantation from AD patients, will be critical to establish causality and define the molecular pathways linking butyrate depletion to neurodegeneration. Diet\u2013microbiome interaction studies should investigate whether dietary patterns (e.g., Mediterranean diet, MIND diet) modulate the butyrate-producing guild and whether this mediates cognitive benefits. Multi-omic integration beyond metabolomics\u2014including transcriptomics, proteomics, and epigenomics in both gut and brain\u2014will provide a more complete picture of the microbiota\u2013gut\u2013brain axis. Clinical trials of targeted butyrate-producing interventions in MCI and early AD patients, with cognitive outcomes and biomarker endpoints, are needed to translate these observational and causal findings into clinical practice. Targeted metabolomic validation of the taurine\u2013bile acid axis, particularly in relation to butyrate-producing consortium depletion, should be prioritized in independent cohorts, given the mediation evidence from our discovery cohort and the neuroprotective properties of taurine demonstrated in preclinical models. Interventional studies of taurine supplementation and hydrogen-based strategies in the AD continuum, informed by the candidate taxa identified here, represent a direct translational extension of this work. Finally, larger and more diverse microbiome GWAS will strengthen MR causal inference and enable investigation of less abundant but functionally important taxa.'))
A(('h2', '4.9 Limitations'))
A(('p', 'This study has several limitations. While our bidirectional two-sample MR analysis using MiBioGen (n=18,340) and IGAP (n=63,926) GWAS data provides causal inference for specific taxa, the cross-sectional design of the discovery and validation cohorts precludes establishing temporal sequence; longitudinal studies are needed. The discovery cohort is relatively small (n=84) and from a single center, though the five-cohort validation meta-analysis (n=755) substantially strengthens generalizability. 16S rRNA sequencing provides genus-level resolution, and strain-level functional differences within genera cannot be assessed; shotgun metagenomics will be needed for finer taxonomic resolution. PICRUSt2 functional prediction is computational inference, not direct metagenomic sequencing, and predicted pathway abundances should be interpreted with caution. The MR analysis is limited to taxa with sufficient genetic instruments in MiBioGen, and less abundant taxa may be underpowered. In addition, the taurine mediation analysis is cross-sectional and rank-based, and its bootstrap confidence interval only marginally excluded zero; it should be regarded as hypothesis-generating. Co-abundance module detection required specifying a cut depth (k=6), although eigengene\u2013stage associations were robust across k=4\u201310, and the high-confidence network threshold (|\u03c1|\u22650.6) was chosen to balance stringency and interpretability, with sensitivity analyses at adjacent thresholds. Fecal metabolite levels reflect luminal rather than systemic concentrations, and the relationship between fecal taurine and blood/CSF taurine requires validation. Hydrogen-guild results were discordant between the discovery cohort (broad H\u2082 producers not significant; H\u2082S producers nominally increased after adjustment) and the validation meta-analysis (both depleted), underscoring that guild behavior can be cohort- and definition-dependent. Finally, the observational associations do not establish causality, and the MR findings, while suggestive, require replication in independent cohorts and functional validation.'))

A(('h1', '5. Conclusions'))
A(('p', 'Integrative multi-omics network analysis, large-scale meta-analysis across five independent validation cohorts, allele-harmonized bidirectional Mendelian randomization, and ASV-level PICRUSt2 functional prediction provide convergent evidence for depleted butyrate- and hydrogen-metabolizing microbial consortia and reduced fecal taurine in AD, with Bifidobacterium as a putative causal risk factor. The strong, covariate-independent, and monotonic decline of a SCFA-enriched protective module from SCD onward, the parallel depletion of acetogenic H\u2082 consumers, and the exploratory Blautia\u2013taurine\u2013cognition mediation signal expand the mechanistic framework for the microbiota\u2013gut\u2013brain axis and identify taurine- and hydrogen-centered microbial metabolism as candidate targets for early intervention in AD.'))

A(('h1', '6. Data Availability'))
A(('p', 'The 16S rRNA gene sequencing and metabolomics data from the discovery cohort will be deposited in public repositories upon acceptance. Validation cohort data are publicly available through NCBI SRA under the accession numbers listed in the Methods. All custom analysis code is available at [GitHub repository URL to be added].'))
A(('h1', '7. Ethics Statement'))
A(('p', 'The discovery cohort study was approved by the local institutional review board (JAMHC-IRB-202201) and conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki. All participants or their legal guardians provided written informed consent prior to enrollment.'))
A(('h1', '8. Funding'))
A(('p', 'This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China [grant numbers to be added] and the Shanghai Municipal Science and Technology Commission [grant numbers to be added].'))
A(('h1', '9. Conflicts of Interest'))
A(('p', 'The authors report no conflicts of interest.'))
A(('h1', '10. Acknowledgments'))
A(('p', 'We thank all participants for their contribution to this study.'))

