TABLE1_NOTE = 'Effect estimates are Spearman r (co-abundance modules and guilds), median normalized intensity (metabolomics), Hedges\u2019 g or Cohen\u2019s d (diversity and functional analyses), mean AUC (machine learning), or IVW estimates (Mendelian randomization). AUC, area under the receiver-operating curve; CI, confidence interval; FDR, false discovery rate; MMSE, Mini-Mental State Examination; MR, Mendelian randomization; NS, not significant; SCFA, short-chain fatty acid. Complete per-genus results are given in Supplementary Table S2; pathway, alpha-diversity, PERMANOVA, and LOCO-CV results in Supplementary Tables S3\u2013S6.'

FIGS = [
 ('Figure 1.', ' Study design schematic. This study employed a two-stage integrative design. Stage 1 (discovery): 84 participants across the AD continuum (HC, SCD, MCI, AD) underwent paired 16S rRNA gene sequencing (V3-V4 region) and high-confidence fecal metabolomics profiling (117 filtered genera; 1,873 metabolites). Co-abundance module analysis identified microbial modules correlated with disease stage and cognitive function; high-confidence microbe\u2013metabolite network analysis, taurine association testing, and rank-based mediation analysis were performed. Stage 2 (validation): five independent publicly available 16S rRNA sequencing cohorts (Beijing, Hangzhou, Lishui, Shanghai, Turkey; n=755 AD/HC fecal samples) were uniformly reprocessed with a standardized DADA2 pipeline and aggregated to genus level. Random-effects meta-analysis, leave-one-cohort-out cross-validation (LOCO-CV) with Lasso regression, SCFA-producing guild analysis, and functional pathway inference were performed. Two-sample bidirectional Mendelian randomization (MR) using MiBioGen (n=18,340) and IGAP AD (n=63,926) GWAS data provided causal inference.'),
 ('Figure 2.', ' Discovery cohort co-abundance module analysis. (A) Protective module M5 eigengene declines progressively across the AD continuum (Spearman r=\u22120.687, p=5.2\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u00b3; ANOVA p=7.1\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u00b9). M5 comprises 33 bacterial genera, 88% Firmicutes, including Faecalibacterium, Roseburia, Blautia, Butyricicoccus, Coprococcus, Agathobacter, and Eubacterium hallii group. (B) High-confidence bipartite microbe\u2013metabolite network (|\u03c1|\u22650.6 and FDR<0.05; 167 edges); the leading hubs (Christensenellaceae R-7 group, UCG-005, Family XIII AD3011 group) are members of the risk-associated module M2. (C) M5 eigengene positively correlates with MMSE score (r=+0.673, p=2.3\u00d710\u207b\u00b9\u00b2), supporting a protective role for this SCFA-enriched consortium.'),
 ('Figure 3.', ' Multi-cohort meta-analysis of differential abundance. (A) Alpha diversity (Shannon index) forest plot across five cohorts, showing no significant pooled difference (Hedges\u2019 g=\u22120.187, p=0.200) but a downward trend in three of five cohorts. (B) Alpha diversity (observed richness) forest plot (g=\u22120.041, p=0.759). (C) All 24 differentially abundant genera (FDR<0.05) from random-effects meta-analysis, ordered by pooled effect size; 10 are AD-enriched (red) and 14 AD-depleted (blue), with pooled coefficients, 95% confidence intervals, and FDR values shown.'),
 ('Figure 4.', ' Beta diversity (Bray-Curtis PCoA) across five validation cohorts. Each panel shows AD (red) vs HC (blue) separation with PERMANOVA R\u00b2 and p-values (999 permutations). Significant separation was observed in Lishui (R\u00b2=0.061, p=0.001), Beijing (R\u00b2=0.019, p=0.001), and Shanghai (R\u00b2=0.172, p=0.001), with marginal significance in Hangzhou (p=0.063) and Turkey (p=0.090).'),
 ('Figure 5.', ' SCFA-producing functional guild and pathway analysis. (A) Random-effects meta-analysis of KEGG functional pathways across four cohorts, showing significant depletion of total SCFA-producing capacity in AD (pooled Cohen\u2019s d=\u22120.524, 95% CI [\u22120.882, \u22120.166], p=0.004, I\u00b2=75.0%). Butanoate metabolism (d=\u22120.502, p=0.080) and propanoate metabolism (d=\u22120.639, p=0.052) showed downward trends. (B) Butanoate metabolism (KEGG map00650) effect sizes by cohort, with significant depletion in Beijing (d=\u22120.743, p=1.9e-12), Lishui (d=\u22121.154, p=2.2e-12), and Hangzhou (d=\u22120.492, p=0.039). (C) Heatmap of SCFA-producing genus effect sizes across five cohorts, showing consistent depletion of butyrate producers (Butyricicoccus, Coprococcus, Roseburia, Faecalibacterium) and enrichment of acetate producers (Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium).'),
 ('Figure 6.', ' Cross-cohort machine learning. (A) Leave-one-cohort-out cross-validation (LOCO-CV) AUC across five cohorts using Lasso-penalized logistic regression, with a mean AUC of 0.656 (range 0.613\u20130.697). (B) Comparison of SCFA-producer-only vs all-genera model performance; SCFA-producing genera alone achieved higher AUC in Lishui (0.767 vs 0.697) and Shanghai (0.698 vs 0.622). (C) Top 15 Lasso discriminative features by mean coefficient across cohorts; Lactobacillus (+0.276), Methanobrevibacter (+0.231), Dialister (\u22120.213), Oscillibacter (+0.209), and Romboutsia (\u22120.186) showed the largest mean coefficients with consistent directionality across held-out cohorts.'),
 ('Figure 7.', ' Two-sample Mendelian randomization analysis. (A) Forest plot of IVW odds ratios (95% CI) for the association between genetically predicted gut microbiota genus abundance and Alzheimer\u2019s disease risk, using MiBioGen (n=18,340) as exposure and IGAP Kunkle 2019 (n=63,926) as outcome. Thirty genera with \u22653 instrumental variables (370 independent SNPs at P<1\u00d710\u207b\u2075, clumped within 500-kb windows) were analyzed. Bifidobacterium shows a significant risk-increasing association (OR=1.306, 95% CI 1.097\u20131.555, p=0.0027); Lachnospiraceae UCG010 shows a suggestive protective association (OR=0.885, p=0.082). (B) Scatter plot of SNP-level effects for Bifidobacterium, with IVW regression line (slope=log(OR)) and 95% CI; each point represents one instrumental variable SNP.'),
 ('Figure 8.', ' Reverse Mendelian randomization analysis (AD\u2192microbiota), allele-harmonized. Forest plot of IVW effect sizes (beta \u00b1 95% CI) for the association between genetically predicted AD liability and gut microbiota genus abundance, using 45 LD-independent AD risk SNPs (P<1\u00d710\u207b\u2075) as instruments and the full MiBioGen genus-level summary statistics (131 genera); 105 genera with \u22653 matched instruments were analyzed after SNP-effect harmonization. Top 20 genera by p-value are shown. Red indicates nominal significance (p<0.05); 13 genera reached nominal significance and one\u2014Bacteroides\u2014survived FDR correction (\u03b2=\u22120.039, p=1.35\u00d710\u207b\u2074, FDR=0.014), indicating that AD liability may reduce Bacteroides carriage. Bifidobacterium (p=0.626) showed no reverse association, supporting the causal direction of the forward MR finding.'),
 ('Figure 9.', ' High-confidence microbe\u2013metabolite bipartite association network in the discovery cohort (n=84). Blue nodes represent bacterial genera (dark blue = SCFA-producing taxa), red nodes represent fecal metabolites (1,873 high-confidence metabolites). Green edges indicate positive Spearman correlations, red edges indicate negative correlations (all |\u03c1|\u22650.6 and FDR<0.05). Node size is proportional to degree. The network comprises 167 significant associations (64 positive, 103 negative) across 21 genera and 86 metabolites. The leading hubs are Christensenellaceae R-7 group (47 edges), UCG-005 (26), Family XIII AD3011 group (16), Oscillospiraceae (15), and Ruminococcaceae (13); the observed mean degree of the top three hubs (29.7) greatly exceeded the permutation null (0.0; 2,000 permutations, p<0.0005). The hub structure was robust at the adjacent threshold (|\u03c1|\u22650.55: 509 edges; same leading hubs).'),
 ('Supplementary Figure S1.', ' Funnel plots for publication bias assessment. Funnel plots of effect size vs. precision (1/SE) for six key differentially abundant genera (Faecalibacterium, Roseburia, Coprococcus, Butyricicoccus, Akkermansia, Lactobacillus). Symmetric distributions support the absence of substantial publication bias, consistent with Egger\u2019s test results (only 6/90 genera with Egger p<0.05).'),
 ('Supplementary Figure S2.', ' Subgroup analysis by sequencing region. Bar chart comparing pooled effect sizes (coef) between V3-V4 (4 cohorts: Hangzhou, Shanghai, Lishui, Turkey; n=355) and V4-V5 (1 cohort: Beijing; n=400) subgroups for eight core SCFA-producing genera. Asterisks indicate p<0.05 within each subgroup. High directional concordance across sequencing regions validates the robustness of the core findings. Faecalibacterium and Roseburia reached significance only in the V4-V5 Beijing cohort.'),
 ('Supplementary Figure S3.', ' Hydrogen-metabolizing microbial guilds in AD. Forest plot of pooled Cohen\u2019s d (95% CI) from random-effects meta-analysis across four cohorts for seven hydrogen-metabolism functional guilds: fermentative H\u2082 producers (40 genera with confirmed hydrogenase pathways), core H\u2082 producers (10 genera), sulfate-reducing bacteria (H\u2082 consumers/H\u2082S producers), acetogens (H\u2082 consumers), total H\u2082 consumers, H\u2082S producers, and the H\u2082 producer-to-consumer balance index. Red markers indicate p<0.05; gray markers indicate p\u22650.05. Fermentative H\u2082 producers (d=\u22120.186, p=0.016, I\u00b2=0%) and H\u2082S producers (d=\u22120.299, p=0.0015, I\u00b2=20.8%) were significantly depleted in AD, while sulfate-reducing bacteria alone showed no significant change (p=0.634). The dashed vertical line indicates zero effect.'),
 ('Supplementary Figure S4.', ' Mendelian randomization sensitivity analyses for Bifidobacterium, Lachnospiraceae UCG010, and Dialister. (A) Bifidobacterium SNP-effect scatter plot with inverse-variance weighted (IVW), weighted median, and MR-Egger regression lines. (B) Bifidobacterium leave-one-SNP-out analysis; odds ratios remain above 1 after removing each instrument. (C) Lachnospiraceae UCG010 scatter plot and (D) leave-one-SNP-out analysis. (E) Dialister scatter plot showing a null association. (F) Odds ratios and 95% confidence intervals from the three MR methods. IVW and weighted median estimates concordantly support a risk-increasing effect of Bifidobacterium (OR\u22481.30), whereas MR-Egger, based on only three instruments, has a very wide confidence interval and is statistically unstable; Cochran\u2019s Q indicates no heterogeneity (p=0.941).'),
 ('Supplementary Figure S5.', ' Cross-cohort consistency of the 24 FDR-significant genera. Heatmap of per-cohort MaAsLin2 effect sizes (AD vs HC) for the 24 pooled-significant genera, ordered by pooled effect size; the left color bar indicates AD-enriched (red) versus AD-depleted (blue) taxa. Asterisks denote cohort-level FDR<0.05; hatched grey cells indicate genera not detected or with insufficient prevalence in that cohort. Seventeen of 24 genera show directionally consistent effects across all cohorts in which they were detected.'),
 ('Supplementary Figure S6.', ' PICRUSt2 functional prediction: top 20 differentially abundant MetaCyc pathways. Forest plot showing pooled Cohen\u2019s d (95% CI) from random-effects meta-analysis across three validation cohorts (Beijing, Lishui, Shanghai; n=631). Red squares indicate pathways depleted in AD (negative d), blue squares indicate pathways enriched in AD (positive d). Significance levels: *FDR<0.05, **FDR<0.01, ***FDR<0.001.'),
 ('Supplementary Figure S7.', ' PICRUSt2 functional prediction: SCFA-related MetaCyc pathways. Forest plot showing pooled Cohen\u2019s d (95% CI) for nine SCFA and fermentation-related pathways from random-effects meta-analysis across three validation cohorts (n=631). Key depleted pathways in AD include methanogenesis from acetate (METH-ACETATE-PWY, FDR=1.4\u00d710\u207b\u2074), propionate fermentation (PWY-5022, FDR=0.048), and butyrate fermentation (PWY-5676, \u2020p=0.048, nominal and not FDR-significant). Significance: *FDR<0.05, **FDR<0.01, ***FDR<0.001, \u2020nominal p<0.05.'),
 ('Supplementary Figure S8.', ' PICRUSt2 functional prediction: cohort-specific effect size heatmap. Heatmap showing Cohen\u2019s d values for the top 15 differentially abundant MetaCyc pathways across three individual validation cohorts (Beijing, Lishui, Shanghai). Red indicates depletion in AD (negative d), blue indicates enrichment in AD (positive d). Values are labeled within each cell. Consistent directionality across cohorts supports the robustness of the functional pathway findings.'),
]
